## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

The whole Protection sub-arc has been about a body you instrument and defend. 4.4 quietly reveals that one variable has been hiding behind almost every problem in the series: **water.** The article opens by tracing the culprit back through everything — *"The bloated, smooth look that hides your conditioning? Water. The blood pressure that climbs on cycle? Largely blood volume — water. The flat, weak, 'stringy' muscle on a hard cut? Too little intracellular water."* The thesis is the controlling image: *"Managing water — not chasing it or starving it — is one of the highest-leverage, least-glamorous things an enhanced athlete does, and the lever you manage it with is electrolytes."* So the poster must be **about fluid**, and the five minerals must read as **levers on that fluid**, not as a supplement shelf.

Four ideas the poster must make a reader *feel* instantly, all of which a generic chat will miss if it just draws five mineral pills:

**1. There are two waters, and they are opposite goals.** The article's bloat-vs-fullness distinction is the entire emotional payload: you want the *intracellular* water HIGH (full, hard, vascular muscle) and the *extracellular* water LOW (no smooth bloat, controlled blood pressure). Drawing the body as **one undifferentiated tank misses the point** — it has to be a **two-compartment vessel** (core you fill, shell you flush) so "the same litre of water in the wrong compartment" is visible. This is what makes electrolytes a *distribution* problem, not a *quantity* problem.

**2. The sodium counterintuition is the hero — and it's a backwards feedback loop.** The article flags it explicitly: *"This is the single most important — and most counterintuitive — idea in the article."* The mechanism is an inversion: cut salt → the body senses low blood volume → **aldosterone rises** → kidneys *cling* to sodium and water → **puffy**. The professional move is the opposite — a *consistent slight sodium surplus* keeps aldosterone **low**, so the kidneys *continuously flush*, and a cheat meal won't blow you up. The right visual device for "doing less of X causes more of X" is a **feedback float valve wired backwards**: the float drops, and instead of opening the drain it *clamps it shut*. This counterintuition is to 4.4 what "same muscle at the nitrogen line" was to 3.1 — the myth-buster rendered as geometry.

**3. Potassium is geared to glycogen, and has the one true red interaction.** The bodybuilding-specific fact: the big intracellular potassium reservoir is *bound to glycogen* — **~10 mg potassium per 1 g carbohydrate** — so a high-carb offseason *needs* proportionally more, and white rice (~1 mg K/g carb) silently under-supplies versus sweet potato (~20 mg/g). That's a **gear ratio**, drawn. And the article's one sharp safety interaction lives here: *"Do not casually mega-dose potassium if you're on an ARB (irbesartan/telmisartan) or an MRA — both raise serum potassium, and stacking high potassium on top risks dangerous hyperkalemia."* That is the poster's single red object — the only place harm is one mistake away.

**4. Magnesium and calcium are an antagonist pair, not two more minerals.** *"Magnesium relaxes, calcium contracts."* Magnesium runs the *eccentric* (lowering, the growth-signal half of the rep); calcium drives the *concentric* (lifting) by binding troponin-C, and magnesium competes for the same sites to relax it afterward. They are a **balance beam** — opposite ends of one mechanism — with magnesium carrying a built-in **self-limiting dose dial** (raise it until loose stool, drop one increment = your absorbable ceiling).

Under those sit two structural truths the instrument must carry: **electrolytes are a ratio scaled to you** (per-bodyweight + per-training-minute + per-gram-carb formulas, with *different target ratios for carb vs keto*), and **electrolytes are the convergence point of the series** — *"they aren't a fixed daily pill — they track your whole protocol,"* so aromatizing/GH, dry-vs-wet compounds, diuretics, clenbuterol, phase, and the **Malaysian climate tax** all feed into the setting. That convergence is *why this is article 4.4, set last.*

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** *Almost every problem in this series is a water problem, and the fix is never chasing or starving water — it's holding the right balance with five levers. You want two opposite waters (intracellular full, extracellular flushed); you keep them by consistency, not restriction; and the master move is the counterintuition — a steady slight sodium surplus keeps aldosterone low so you flush, while cutting salt clamps the drain shut and bloats you. Potassium rides with your carbs (but never mega-dosed on an ARB), magnesium and calcium balance the relax/contract halves of every rep, and the whole setting is a ratio scaled to your bodyweight, your training, your phase, and your climate — set last, because everything upstream moves it.*

