## 1. The Big Idea

_Almost every problem in this series is a **water** problem — and electrolytes are the five levers that manage it. The poster is a **fluid-balance regulation stand**: the body drawn as a **two-compartment vessel** — a bright **intracellular core you keep FULL** (fullness, pump, hard muscle) inside a dim **extracellular shell you keep LOW** (bloat, blood-pressure water) — with water entering through a **steady, consistency-governed inlet** (sip, don't chug-and-starve). Around the vessel sit five labelled levers. The hero is the **sodium drain on a backwards float valve**: the aldosterone loop, where *cutting* salt makes the float read "empty" and **clamps the drain shut** (puffy), while a *consistent slight surplus* keeps the drain **open and flushing** (dry, and a cheat meal won't blow you up). **Potassium** is a fill valve **geared to a glycogen tank** (~10 mg per gram of carb) — and it carries the page's one red object, a **hyperkalemia interlock** that locks out mega-dosing when an ARB/MRA is in the stack. **Magnesium and calcium** ride an **antagonist balance beam** (magnesium relaxes the eccentric, calcium drives the concentric), with a self-limiting magnesium dial. At the foot, a **per-bodyweight formula plate** with a **carb ⇄ keto ratio selector** sets the targets, and a **convergence manifold** shows every upstream decision — aromatizing compounds, dry vs wet, diuretics, clenbuterol, phase, and the **Malaysian heat** — feeding into the lever settings. The instrument's argument: **you don't chase or starve water — you regulate its balance, and you set it last because everything moves it.**_

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure

A **two-compartment hydraulic regulation stand** — the visual language of a fluid vessel with feedback-controlled valves, a balance beam, and a calibrated setpoint plate. It earns its place six ways:

1. **Two waters become two compartments, drawn.** The body is a tall vessel with a **bright, sharply-detailed inner CORE** (intracellular — labelled `KEEP FULL · fullness · pump · hard muscle`) suspended inside a **dim outer SHELL** (extracellular — labelled `KEEP LOW · bloat · blood-volume · BP water`). The reader sees instantly that the goal isn't *less water* or *more water* — it's **water in the right compartment**. A flat/cut state is shown as the core going slack (the "stringy muscle" failure) when intracellular water drops; a bloated state is the shell swelling. This is the distribution-not-quantity insight as a single shape.

2. **The sodium counterintuition becomes a backwards float valve (the hero).** The vessel's main **DRAIN** is governed by a **float valve labelled `ALDOSTERONE (RAAS)`**, and it is wired *against intuition*. A small inset shows the paradox as **two identical vessels side by side**:
   - **LEFT — "CUT SALT TO LOOK DRY":** sodium lever pulled low → float reads *low volume* → **aldosterone HIGH** → drain **CLAMPS SHUT** → shell stays **PUFFY**. Caption: `less salt → MORE water. backfires.`
   - **RIGHT — "CONSISTENT SLIGHT SURPLUS":** sodium lever held steady-high → **aldosterone LOW** → drain **OPEN, continuously flushing** → shell runs **DRY**, and a small `CHEAT / REFEED` input just drains straight through. Caption: `steady surplus → low aldosterone → you flush.`
   The inverted float (drop → *close*, not open) is the visual myth-buster. A grace note ties the BP ancillaries in: a small tag `tadalafil · telmisartan · nebivolol → keep the blood volume, control the pressure` on the surplus side.

3. **Potassium becomes a glycogen-geared fill valve — and the red interlock.** The **POTASSIUM** lever fills the intracellular core, but it is **coupled by a visible gear to a GLYCOGEN TANK** stamped `~10 mg K per 1 g carb`: turn the carb tank up (offseason) and the potassium valve must open proportionally to stay full; a low-carb/keto tank shrinks it. A small **food-source dial** shows the gearing differs by source — `sweet potato ~20 mg/g · white rice ~1 mg/g` — so a white-rice staple reads as an under-supplied valve needing a top-up (`+spinach / K-chloride salt`). The **single red object on the page** is a **HYPERKALEMIA INTERLOCK** clamped on this valve: `ARB (irbesartan/telmisartan) or MRA in the stack → DO NOT mega-dose K · food first · confirm on bloodwork`, drawn as a hard red lockout because both the drug and the dose push serum potassium the same way and stacking them is the one move that can stop a heart. Red appears *nowhere else.*

4. **Magnesium and calcium become an antagonist balance beam.** A horizontal **BALANCE BEAM** carries the rep itself: **MAGNESIUM** on one end (`RELAX · the eccentric · lowering · the growth half`), **CALCIUM** on the other (`CONTRACT · the concentric · troponin-C · force`) — opposite ends of one mechanism, competing for the same sites. Magnesium's end carries a **self-limiting dose dial** with a printed rule `raise until loose stool → drop one increment = your ceiling · glycinate/citrate, not oxide`. Calcium's end carries `timed-release, don't mega-dose in one hit · pulled from bone if chronically low`. The beam makes them read as a *pair tuning one motion*, not two more pills.

5. **The setpoint plate becomes a per-bodyweight formula nomogram + ratio selector.** At the foot, a **calibrated FORMULA PLATE** renders the five working formulas as input dials feeding output targets:
   - `WATER 500–1,000 ml / 10 kg` · `SODIUM ~500 mg / litre water` · `POTASSIUM 500 mg/10 kg + 10 mg/g carb` · `MAGNESIUM 100 mg/10 kg + 10–15 mg/min trained` · `CALCIUM 100 mg/10 kg + 5 mg/min trained`.
   A **RATIO SELECTOR toggle** sits beside it with two presets — **CARB** `~2–2.5 Na : 5–5.5 K : 1.5 Mg : 1 Ca` and **KETO** `~2.5–3 Na : 4–4.5 K : 2 Mg : 1–1.5 Ca` — physically *re-proportioning* the lever heights when flipped (carbs raise the K share; keto raises the Na/Mg share). A small worked-example chip: `90 kg · ~300 g carb · ~100 min/day · hot climate → water ~7–9 L · Na ~4,000 · K ~7,500 · Mg ~1,900–2,300 · Ca ~1,400`.

6. **The convergence manifold draws "set it last."** Feeding into the whole stand from the side is a **MANIFOLD of upstream inputs**, each a labelled pipe that nudges a lever: `aromatizing + GH → ↑Na/water (lean on consistent Na + BP ancillaries)` · `dry vs wet compound → different water feel, same athlete` · `diuretics (indapamide) → dumps Na AND K — why crashing electrolytes is dangerous, not clever` · `clenbuterol → burns K + taurine` · `carb vs keto / hard cut → moves the ratio + drops intracellular water` · `bodyweight · sweat · MALAYSIAN HEAT → top of every range`. A printed law plate on the manifold: `ELECTROLYTES TRACK YOUR WHOLE PROTOCOL — SET THEM LAST, REVISIT THEM OFTEN.` This is the structural reason the article is 4.4, made visible.

**The signature single object** is the **inverted aldosterone float valve** — the one place the instrument behaves *backwards on purpose*. Where 3.1's signature was the equal-height nitrogen datum and 4.3's was the no-dose redline kill-zone, 4.4's is the float that **clamps shut when it should open**, with the paired side-by-side vessels proving the paradox in one glance. The **consistency-governed inlet** is a quiet second signature: a steady metered drip labelled `sip steadily` next to a ghosted, crossed-out `chug-and-starve → vasopressin clamps filtration`.

This is a deliberately *different instrument* from every prior PE poster — the only hydraulic/fluid one — but speaks the same locked "Engineering Diagnostic Instrument" language: the regulation stand the water balance actually runs on.

